{"id":"mica","name":"Mica","formula":"K(Mg,Fe)₃AlSi₃O₁₀(OH)₂","chronometers":"⁴⁰Ar/³⁹Ar, K–Ar, Rb–Sr","system":"Monoclinic","point_group":"mmm","setting":"orthohexagonal pseudo-cell (a = b/√3, β = 90°)","lattice":{"a":5.3289,"b":9.23,"c":10.17,"alpha":90.0,"beta":90.0,"gamma":90.0},"lattice_source":"orthohexagonal pseudo-cell from biotite-1M (Handbook of Mineralogy)","elongation_axis":[0,0,1],"mono_color":"#4a3628","view":{"tilt":38,"turn":-20},"forms":[{"key":"001","label":"{001}","index":[0,0,1],"name":"basal pinacoid c","color":"#3b2d22"},{"key":"110","label":"{110}","index":[1,1,0],"name":"prism","color":"#7a5a3a"},{"key":"010","label":"{010}","index":[0,1,0],"name":"side pinacoid b","color":"#a98458"}],"matrix":{"title":"Plate outline × thickness","subtitle":"Basal {001} plates bounded by {110} and {010}. Rows run from thick books to thin flakes.","row_title":"Thickness : width","col_title":"Plate outline","rows":[{"label":"1:2","detail":""},{"label":"1:3","detail":""},{"label":"1:7","detail":""},{"label":"1:17","detail":""}],"cols":[{"label":"S1","detail":"{110} only"},{"label":"S2","detail":"{110} > {010}"},{"label":"S3","detail":"{110} > {010}"},{"label":"S4","detail":"{110} > {010}"}],"note":""},"about":"\n<p>Biotite and muscovite are workhorse <sup>40</sup>Ar/<sup>39</sup>Ar and Rb–Sr thermochronometers. Their habit is a\npseudo-hexagonal plate: a dominant basal pinacoid c{001} bounded by b{010} and m{110}. Muscovite can be a rhombic\nplate of c + m only. The plate radius is generally taken as the Ar diffusion domain, modelled as a cylinder\n(Hames &amp; Bowring 1994; Harrison et al. 2009).</p>\n<p>The true 1M cell (C2/m, β ≈ 100°) puts {110} at an angle to (001). For rendering, the plates here use an\northohexagonal pseudo-cell instead: a = b/√3, β = 90°, c = 10.17 Å, with point group mmm. This gives right\npseudo-hexagonal prisms, which is a modelling choice.</p>\n","habits":[{"name":"Pseudo-hexagonal plate","text":"c ≫ b and m (column S3 is close to a regular hexagon)."},{"name":"Book","text":"Thick tabular or short-prismatic, with c comparable to b and m (top row)."},{"name":"Rhombic plate","text":"c + m only, typical of muscovite (column S1)."},{"name":"Not modelled","text":"Tapering prismatic phlogopite with steep pyramids (their indices are uncertain) and mica-law twins."}],"references":[{"citation":"Brigatti, M.F., Guggenheim, S. (2002). Mica crystal chemistry and the influence of pressure, temperature, and solid solution on atomistic models. <i>Reviews in Mineralogy and Geochemistry</i> 46, 1–97.","doi":"10.2138/rmg.2002.46.01","url":null,"note":"Mica crystal chemistry.","tags":["review"]},{"citation":"Hames, W.E., Bowring, S.A. (1994). An empirical evaluation of the argon diffusion geometry in muscovite. <i>Earth and Planetary Science Letters</i> 124, 161–169.","doi":"10.1016/0012-821X(94)00079-4","url":null,"note":"Shows the Ar diffusion domain is approximately the physical grain.","tags":["thermochronology","geometry"]},{"citation":"Harrison, T.M., Célérier, J., Aikman, A.B., Hermann, J., Heizler, M.T. (2009). Diffusion of <sup>40</sup>Ar in muscovite. <i>Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta</i> 73, 1039–1051.","doi":"10.1016/j.gca.2008.09.038","url":null,"note":"Ar diffusion in muscovite using cylindrical geometry.","tags":["thermochronology","geometry"]},{"citation":"Harrison, T.M., Duncan, I., McDougall, I. (1985). Diffusion of <sup>40</sup>Ar in biotite: temperature, pressure and compositional effects. <i>Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta</i> 49, 2461–2468.","doi":"10.1016/0016-7037(85)90246-7","url":null,"note":"Ar diffusion in biotite.","tags":["thermochronology"]},{"citation":"Grove, M., Harrison, T.M. (1996). <sup>40</sup>Ar* diffusion in Fe-rich biotite. <i>American Mineralogist</i> 81, 940–951.","doi":"10.2138/am-1996-7-816","url":null,"note":"Composition dependence of Ar diffusion in biotite.","tags":["thermochronology"]},{"citation":"Kirschner, D.L., Cosca, M.A., Masson, H., Hunziker, J.C. (1996). Staircase <sup>40</sup>Ar/<sup>39</sup>Ar spectra of fine-grained white mica: timing and duration of deformation and empirical constraints on argon diffusion. <i>Geology</i> 24, 747–750.","doi":"10.1130/0091-7613(1996)024<0747:SAASOF>2.3.CO;2","url":null,"note":"Grain size as the diffusion domain in fine white mica.","tags":["thermochronology","geometry"]},{"citation":"Lovera, O.M., Richter, F.M., Harrison, T.M. (1989). The <sup>40</sup>Ar/<sup>39</sup>Ar thermochronometry for slowly cooled samples having a distribution of diffusion domain sizes. <i>Journal of Geophysical Research</i> 94, 17917–17935.","doi":"10.1029/JB094iB12p17917","url":null,"note":"The multi-domain diffusion (MDD) model.","tags":["thermochronology"]}],"cells":[{"id":"T1_S1","row":0,"col":0,"label":"S1","sub":"1:2","description":"Outline: {110} only. Thickness:width ≈ 1:1.7.","default":false,"params":{"face_distances":{"001":0.02,"110":1.0,"010":null},"cap_translation":1.18},"fractions":{"001":0.2941,"110":0.7059},"metrics":{"volume":1.0,"surface_area":6.31749,"rsv":0.47487,"sphericity":0.76549,"aspect":0.6},"geometry":{"vertices":[[-0.51787,-0.0,-0.53819],[0.0,0.89698,-0.53819],[0.0,-0.89698,-0.53819],[0.51787,0.0,-0.53819],[0.0,0.89698,0.53819],[-0.51787,-0.0,0.53819],[0.51787,0.0,0.53819],[0.0,-0.89698,0.53819]],"faces":[{"form":"001","hkl":[0,0,1],"loop":[6,4,5,7]},{"form":"001","hkl":[0,0,-1],"loop":[3,2,0,1]},{"form":"110","hkl":[1,1,0],"loop":[6,3,1,4]},{"form":"110","hkl":[-1,-1,0],"loop":[5,0,2,7]},{"form":"110","hkl":[-1,1,0],"loop":[1,0,5,4]},{"form":"110","hkl":[1,-1,0],"loop":[6,7,2,3]}]},"files":{"stl":"models/stl/mica/mica_T1_S1.stl","step":"models/step/mica/mica_T1_S1.step"}},{"id":"T1_S2","row":0,"col":1,"label":"S2","sub":"1:2","description":"Outline: {110} > {010}. Thickness:width ≈ 1:1.7.","default":false,"params":{"face_distances":{"001":0.02,"110":1.0,"010":1.333333},"cap_translation":0.812512},"fractions":{"001":0.3905,"110":0.4876,"010":0.1219},"metrics":{"volume":1.0,"surfa